// Cs8.grammar — C# 8.0: switch expressions (T3.8.1).
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives (declaration order across
// files); it does NOT modify the Cs1 alternative. The engine is longest-match-wins and an
// equal-length tie resolves to the FIRST alternative (declaration order, Parser.cs:296-316). So a
// re-declared Cs8 alternative is reachable only when it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the existing ones.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser):
//   * Structure — ParseSwitchExpression (LanguageParser_Patterns.cs:593-658):
//     SwitchExpression(governingExpression, switchKeyword, OpenBrace, arms, CloseBrace); arms is a
//     comma-separated list of SwitchExpressionArm(pattern, whenClause, =>, expression) (629-637). The
//     arm pattern is ParsePattern(Precedence.Coalescing); the arm value is ParseExpressionCore().
//   * Operator recognition — GetExpressionOperatorTokenKindAndExpressionKind (LanguageParser.cs:11782):
//     a `switch` token followed by `{` yields SyntaxKind.SwitchExpression; it is formed in the
//     operator loop (ParseExpressionContinued.tryExpandExpression, 11557-11612), i.e. a POSTFIX-like
//     operator on the governing expression.
//   * Precedence — GetPrecedence (LanguageParser.cs:11271-11273): SwitchExpression -> Precedence.Switch
//     (the enum, 11197-11221, orders ... Additive, Multiplicative, Switch, Range, Unary, Cast, ...
//     Primary). The switch expression binds TIGHTER than every binary operator; the governing
//     expression is parsed at the Switch precedence (a "Unary-tighter" expression).
//   * Version gate — IDS_FeatureSwitchExpression (Errors/MessageID.cs:191, :631-634) ->
//     LanguageVersion.CSharp8 (a BINDER/semantic check, Binder_Statements.cs:396).
//
// CS8 only.

// === Switch expression (C# 8.0). Re-declares Cs1 Expression (append, T0.3 merge) as a TDOPP POSTFIX
// operator at the Unary level (the nearest existing level tighter than every binary operator; Roslyn
// Precedence.Switch sits between Multiplicative and Unary). The operand is the PREFIX (a primary-ish
// expression: Primary / unary / cast) — the postfix `rest` is "switch" "{" ... "}" (Parser.cs:141).
// It REQUIRES the reserved "switch" keyword (followed by "{") after the operand (REQUIRED-new-construct),
// so it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with every existing postfix (none starts with "switch"; "switch" is
// reserved, Cs1 ReservedKeyword:603). Roslyn ParseSwitchExpression (LanguageParser_Patterns.cs:593).
// ===
Expression =
    | SwitchExpression = Expression : Unary "switch" "{" SwitchArm ("," SwitchArm)* "}";

// A switch expression arm: Pattern "=>" Expression (Roslyn SwitchExpressionArm,
// LanguageParser_Patterns.cs:629). The Pattern is the T3.6.2 Pattern rule (type/constant/declaration/
// discard/var). The arm value is `Expression : Comma` (minPrecedence = Comma bp 1) so it does NOT
// absorb the "," arm separator via the Comma operator (the TDOPP Comma fix, T3.3.2 — the same
// mechanism Cs4 Argument uses). Single-sequence rule ending in a precedence-qualified RuleRef (a
// SimpleRule, not an anonymous alternative, not in a group — the two T3.3.2 meta-grammar constraints).
SwitchArm = Pattern "=>" Expression : Comma;

// ============================================================================
// T3.8.2 — C# 8.0 using declarations.
//
// A using declaration is a NEW STATEMENT form: `using Type Identifier = Expression ;` (or
// `using var Identifier = Expression ;`). It is a local-variable-declaration-like statement with a
// leading `using` keyword (automatic resource disposal at the end of the scope).
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp\Portable):
//   * Structure — ParseLocalDeclarationStatement (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:10482-10579): a using
//     declaration is a LocalDeclarationStatement with a usingKeyword (and optional awaitKeyword). If
//     the current token is UsingKeyword it is eaten (10491-10495), then modifiers + type + variables
//     + Semicolon. Form: `[await] using` + modifiers? + type + variables + `;`. The task's form is the
//     single-declarator-with-initializer case (`using T id = expr ;` / `using var id = expr ;`).
//   * Version gate — IDS_FeatureUsingDeclarations (Errors/MessageID.cs:182, `// semantic check` :620),
//     checked in Binder/UsingStatementBinder.cs:98 via CheckFeatureAvailability — a BINDER (semantic)
//     check, C# 8.0. The PARSER accepts the form wherever the rule exists (CS8 only, per the plan).
//   * Version-purity test — UsingDeclarationTests.cs:830 (UsingDeclarationsWithLangVer7_3): `using
//     IDisposable x = null;` gives CS8652 at C# 7.3, parses at C# 8.0.
//   * The `using` STATEMENT (`using ( ... ) ...`) is C# 1.0 (Cs1 UsingStatement, :935) — UNCHANGED.
//
// CS8 only.

// === Using declaration (C# 8.0). Re-declares Cs1 Statement (append, T0.3 merge). The new
// alternative REQUIRES the reserved "using" keyword followed by a type (or "var") + identifier +
// "=" + expression + ";" (REQUIRED-new-construct). Roslyn ParseLocalDeclarationStatement
// (LanguageParser.cs:10482). ===
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * vs UsingStatement ("using" "(" ResourceAcquisition ")" Statement, Cs1:935): after "using", the
//     using declaration has a type/var while the using statement has "(". A token is either "(" or a
//     type/var, not both -> mutually exclusive.
//   * vs every other Statement alternative: "using" is reserved (Cs1 ReservedKeyword:614), so it is
//     NOT an IdentifierName and NO other Statement alternative starts with it.
//   * vs LocalVariableDeclaration / ExpressionStatement / LabeledStatement: the reserved "using" is
//     not a Type/expression-start/identifier -> they all FAIL.
//
// Version purity: at v7 the UsingDeclaration is ABSENT (Cs8-only), so `using FileStream f = ...`
// REJECTS (UsingStatement fails on the missing "("; no other Statement starts with the reserved
// "using"). At v8 it PARSES. The using STATEMENT is untouched (no regression at v1-v7).
Statement =
    | UsingDeclaration = "using" UsingDeclarationType !ReservedKeyword Identifier "=" Expression ";";

// The using declaration's type: a real type OR the "var" keyword. Named union (the meta-grammar
// forbids `|` inside a group). "var" is reserved at v2+ (Cs2:186-187), so it is NOT a TypeName
// (TypeName = !ReservedKeyword Identifier) and Type NEVER matches "var" -> the two alternatives are
// mutually exclusive (no equal-length tie). Roslyn: the variable type is `var` or an explicit type
// (ParseLocalDeclaration, LanguageParser.cs:10517).
UsingDeclarationType =
    | Type
    | "var";

// ============================================================================
// T3.8.3a — C# 8.0 index-from-end `^` prefix operator.
//
// The index-from-end operator `^` (C# 8.0): `x[^1]` — a `^` PREFIX operator producing an index-from-end
// expression, used as an indexer argument (the `[]` postfix, Cs1.grammar:732 `Indexer = "[" (Expression;
// ",")* "]"`). The `^` is a NEW TDOPP PREFIX operator on `Expression` (the SAME shape as the Cs5 `await`
// prefix, Cs5.grammar:74, and the Cs7 `ref`/`throw` prefixes, Cs7.grammar:295/362).
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp\Portable):
//   * Form + precedence — GetPrefixUnaryExpression (Syntax/SyntaxKindFacts.cs:416-441): CaretToken ->
//     IndexExpression (436-437). IsExpectedPrefixUnaryOperator (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:11356-11359) is
//     true for `^` (a prefix unary, not ref/out), so `^` is parsed in parseUnaryOrPrimaryExpression
//     (11463-11473) as a PrefixUnaryExpression with operand ParseSubExpression(GetPrecedence(Index-
//     Expression)); GetPrecedence (11300-11301): IndexExpression -> Precedence.Unary — the SAME level as
//     AwaitExpression (11299) and every other unary prefix operator.
//   * The `^` BITWISE-XOR (the OTHER role of the `^` symbol) — GetBinaryExpression (SyntaxKindFacts.cs:
//     645-658): CaretToken -> ExclusiveOrExpression. So `^` is BOTH a prefix unary (index-from-end, CS8)
//     and a binary operator (XOR, CS1). Disambiguated by POSITION (prefix = no left operand vs infix = a
//     left operand present), which maps onto this grammar's TDOPP prefix (IndexExpr) vs postfix (BitXor,
//     Cs1.grammar:667) split.
//   * Version gate — IDS_FeatureIndexOperator (Errors/MessageID.cs:177, "C# 8.0 features" block :616 ->
//     LanguageVersion.CSharp8 :634, `// semantic check`), checked in Binder/Expressions/Binder_
//     Expressions.cs:2683 — a BINDER (semantic) check. The PARSER accepts the form wherever the rule
//     exists (CS8 only, per the plan).
//   * Syntax tests — Test/Syntax/Parsing/ExpressionParsingTests.cs: IndexExpression (5280, `^1`),
//     RangeExpression_Binary_WithIndexes (5337, `^5..^3` — the `..` range is T3.8.3b; only the `^` bounds
//     are in scope here).
//
// CS8 only.

// === Index-from-end `^` (C# 8.0). Re-declares Cs1 Expression (append, T0.3 merge); APPENDS the IndexExpr
// prefix. A `^` PREFIX operator (like the Cs5 `await`, Cs5.grammar:74, and the Cs7 `ref`/`throw`,
// Cs7.grammar:295/362): `^` + expression. It is a PREFIX alternative (it starts with the "^" literal, NOT
// a self-Ref to Expression, so the TDOPP builder classifies it as a prefix, Parser.cs:137-149) and is
// tried among the prefix alternatives at the START of an expression. The operand is `Expression : Unary`
// (a ReqRef with Precedence = the Unary level, RuleGenerator.cs:90-94): minPrecedence = Unary's binding
// power (15), so the operand binds tighter than all binary operators (Multiplicative bp 14 and below) —
// `^ x + y` = `(^ x) + y` — and the Comma (binding power 1, the lowest) is NOT applicable, so the operand
// does NOT absorb a following `,` in a comma-separated context (e.g. an indexer argument list `x[^i, ^j]`).
// This is the TDOPP Comma fix (T3.3.2) handled by the `: Unary` precedence — no separate `Expression :
// Comma` is needed. Roslyn GetPrefixUnaryExpression (SyntaxKindFacts.cs:436-437) + GetPrecedence
// (LanguageParser.cs:11300-11301, IndexExpression -> Precedence.Unary). ===
//
// Disambiguation with the `^` BITWISE-XOR (longest-match, no equal-length tie): the `^` PREFIX (IndexExpr)
// and the `^` BINARY (BitXor postfix, Cs1.grammar:667) are in DIFFERENT TDOPP phases — the prefix is tried
// at the START of an expression (no left operand), the postfix is applied AFTER a left operand. They are
// mutually exclusive:
//   * `^1` (index-from-end): PrimaryExpr FAILS (no Primary matches the `^` symbol); IndexExpr -> `^ 1`.
//     Sole successful prefix.
//   * `x ^ y` (bitwise-XOR): PrimaryExpr -> `x`; IndexExpr FAILS (the start is `x`, not `^`). The BitXor
//     POSTFIX (bp LogicalXor) then applies to `x` -> `x ^ y`. The `^` prefix is NEVER tried.
//   * `x ^ ^1`: BitXor postfix on `x`; its RHS (Expression : LogicalXor) starts with `^` -> IndexExpr.
//     -> `x ^ (^1)` (index-from-end binds tighter than XOR; Roslyn Unary > LogicalXor).
//
// Version purity: at v7 the IndexExpr prefix is ABSENT, so `x[^1]` REJECTS (the indexer's inner Expression
// starts with `^`; PrimaryExpr fails, no other prefix matches `^`; the BitXor POSTFIX requires a left
// operand and cannot start the expression). At v8 it PARSES. The bitwise-XOR (`x ^ y`) is UNCHANGED at
// v1-v8 (the `^` prefix is absent at v1-v7; at v8 it only applies at the START of an expression, so it does
// not interfere with `x ^ y`) — no regression.
Expression =
    | IndexExpr = "^" Expression : Unary;

// ============================================================================
// T3.8.3b — C# 8.0 range operator `..` (BINARY form only: `left .. right`, both operands present).
//
// The BINARY range `1 .. 2` (C# 8.0) is a TDOPP POSTFIX (binary) operator on `Expression` at the NEW
// `Range` precedence level (added to the Cs1 precedence list, between `Unary` and `Multiplicative` —
// Roslyn Precedence.Range, LanguageParser.cs:11215, orders … Multiplicative, Switch, Range, Unary,
// Cast …). Only the binary form is in scope here; the end-only (`.. 2`) and start-only (`2 ..`) forms
// are separate sub-points (T3.8.3c / T3.8.3d).
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp\Portable):
//   * Form + precedence — GetPrecedence (LanguageParser.cs:11308-11309): RangeExpression ->
//     Precedence.Range. The binary form is a POSTFIX (the `..` is applied after the left operand):
//     ParseExpressionContinued.tryExpandExpression (LanguageParser.cs:11617-11625) forms
//     RangeExpression(leftOperand, .., ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Range)).
//   * Operator recognition — GetExpressionOperatorTokenKindAndExpressionKind (LanguageParser.cs:11744):
//     `..` -> (DotDotToken, RangeExpression).
//   * Version gate — CS8 (the plan's boundary; Roslyn's is a binder/semantic check).
//   * Syntax test — Test/Syntax/Parsing/ExpressionParsingTests.cs: RangeExpression_Binary (5318, `1..2`).
//
// `..` is a 2-char LITERAL (a `StartsWith` match, Rules.cs:76-77) — the SAME mechanism as the 1-char `.`
// literal used by `MemberAccess = "." Identifier` (Cs1.grammar:731). It is NOT a terminal (no `DotDot`
// terminal is added). The `.` member-access lives in the `PostfixOp*` loop (a DIFFERENT phase, run while
// resolving the prefix) and REQUIRES an Identifier after the single `.`, so a bare `..` (second char is
// `.`, not an identifier) never matches `MemberAccess` -> the two are mutually exclusive. The `..` literal
// is matched only by this RangeBinary postfix (no other postfix starts with `..`).
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match-wins; the `1 .` real-literal trap is AVOIDED by the required spaces in
// `1 .. 2`): for `1 .. 2`, the space after `1` stops `DecimalRealLiteral` (`[0-9]+\.[0-9]*`) from matching
// `1.`, so the left operand is `DecInt`=`1`; then the RangeBinary postfix matches `.. 2`. (The no-space
// form `1..2` is out of scope — see the progress doc for the `Real`/`1.` boundary.)
//
// Version purity: at v7 the RangeBinary postfix is ABSENT, so `1 .. 2` REJECTS (the left operand `1` is
// parsed, then no postfix consumes `..` and the statement sees `..` where `;` is expected). At v8 it
// PARSES. The `.` member-access (`x.y`) is UNCHANGED at v1-v8 (the `..` literal needs two dots; `x.y` has
// one) — no regression.
Expression =
    | RangeBinary = Expression : Range ".." Expression : Range;

// ============================================================================
// T3.8.3c — C# 8.0 range operator `..` (UNARY PREFIX form only: `.. right`, no left operand).
//
// The UNARY PREFIX range `.. 2` (C# 8.0) is a TDOPP PREFIX operator on `Expression` at the `Range`
// precedence level (the SAME level the T3.8.3b binary `RangeBinary` uses; Roslyn Precedence.Range,
// LanguageParser.cs:11308-11309). The prefix `..` is parsed in parseUnaryOrPrimaryExpression
// (LanguageParser.cs:11478-11486): a `..` token with leftOperand: null and a right operand
// ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Range). This is the end-only form (T3.8.3c); the start-only form
// (`2 ..`) is T3.8.3d.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp\Portable):
//   * Form + precedence — parseUnaryOrPrimaryExpression (LanguageParser.cs:11478-11486):
//     IsAtDotDotToken() -> RangeExpression(leftOperand: null, EatDotDotToken(),
//     ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Range)). GetPrecedence (11308-11309): RangeExpression ->
//     Precedence.Range. The `..` is a PREFIX unary (no left operand), NOT a binary operator.
//   * Precedence ordering — Precedence enum (LanguageParser.cs:11197-11221): … Multiplicative, Switch,
//     Range, Unary, Cast … — Range sits between Multiplicative and Unary (tighter than Multiplicative,
//     looser than Unary). The CsNitra `Range` level (Cs1.grammar, added in T3.8.3b) is the SAME level.
//   * Associativity — a PREFIX unary operator has no associativity flag (its single operand is on the
//     right); the operand binds at Precedence.Range (ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Range)), so it
//     absorbs only operators tighter than Range (Unary, Cast, primary/postfix) and stops at Range or
//     looser.
//   * Version gate — CS8 (the plan's boundary; Roslyn's is a binder/semantic check).
//
// `..` is the SAME 2-char LITERAL as the T3.8.3b binary form (a `StartsWith` match, Rules.cs:76-77).
// NOT a terminal. The prefix is tried at the START of an expression (no left operand); the T3.8.3b
// binary `RangeBinary` is a POSTFIX (a left operand is present) — DIFFERENT TDOPP phases, so they are
// mutually exclusive by position: `.. 2` (starts with `..`) -> RangePrefix; `1 .. 2` (starts with `1`)
// -> PrimaryExpr `1` + RangeBinary postfix. The `.` member-access (one dot + Identifier,
// Cs1.grammar:731) needs an Identifier after the single `.`, so a bare `..` never matches it (no
// regression).
//
// Version purity: at v7 the RangePrefix is ABSENT, so `.. 2` REJECTS (at the start, PrimaryExpr fails —
// no Primary matches `..` — and no other prefix starts with `..`). At v8 it PARSES.
Expression =
    | RangePrefix = ".." Expression : Range;

// ============================================================================
// T3.8.3d — C# 8.0 range operator `..` (UNARY POSTFIX form only: `left ..`, no right operand).
//
// The UNARY POSTFIX range `2 ..` (C# 8.0, the start-only form) is a TDOPP POSTFIX operator on `Expression`
// at the SAME `Range` precedence level the T3.8.3b binary `RangeBinary` uses (Roslyn Precedence.Range,
// LanguageParser.cs:11308-11309). The start-only form is parsed in ParseExpressionContinued.
// tryExpandExpression (LanguageParser.cs:11617-11625): `RangeExpression(leftOperand, ..,
// CanStartExpression() ? ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Range) : null)` — when nothing expression-start
// follows the `..`, the right operand is null (the start-only form). The `..` is applied AFTER the left
// operand (a POSTFIX); there is NO right operand. Associativity: `RangeExpression` is not in Roslyn's
// IsRightAssociative list (11173-11195) — the binary form is left-associative; the postfix form has no
// right operand so no associativity applies.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp\Portable):
//   * Form + precedence — tryExpandExpression (LanguageParser.cs:11617-11625): the right operand is
//     `ParseSubExpression(Precedence.Range)` when `CanStartExpression()` else `null`. GetPrecedence
//     (11308-11309): RangeExpression -> Precedence.Range.
//   * Operator recognition — GetExpressionOperatorTokenKindAndExpressionKind (LanguageParser.cs:
//     11742-11744): `..` -> (DotDotToken, RangeExpression).
//   * Version gate — CS8 (the plan's boundary; Roslyn's is a binder/semantic check).
//
// `..` is the SAME 2-char LITERAL as the T3.8.3b/T3.8.3c forms (a `StartsWith` match, Rules.cs:76-77).
// NOT a terminal. This is a POSTFIX (a self-Ref to Expression as the first element, Parser.cs:137-146):
// the builder strips the `Expression : Range` self-Ref, and since the `rest` (`[".."]`) has no ReqRef it
// falls back to the first element's precedence (`Range`, Parser.cs:144-145) and emits a postfix matching
// the bare `..` (no right operand).
//
// Disambiguation with the T3.8.3b BINARY form (longest-match-wins, Parser.cs:386): both are POSTFIX at the
// `Range` level. For `2 .. 3` the `RangeBinary` postfix consumes `2 .. 3` (longer) and wins; for `2 ..`
// (nothing after) `RangeBinary` FAILS (no right operand after the `..`) and the `RangePostfix` postfix
// matches the bare `..` -> `2 ..`. The `.` member-access (one dot + Identifier, Cs1.grammar:731, a
// PostfixOp-loop postfix) needs an Identifier after the single `.`, so a bare `..` never matches it (no
// regression).
//
// Version purity: at v7 the RangePostfix is ABSENT, so `2 ..` REJECTS (the left operand `2` is parsed,
// then no postfix consumes the trailing `..` and the statement sees `..` where `;` is expected). At v8 it
// PARSES.
Expression =
    | RangePostfix = Expression : Range "..";
